---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import GasExchangeSim from '../../simulators/gasexchange/GasExchangeSim.astro';
import { gasExchangeQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/gasexchange';

const toc = [
  { id: 'why', label: 'Why animals need gas exchange' },
  { id: 'diffusion', label: 'Diffusion and Fick’s law' },
  { id: 'water-air', label: 'Water versus air' },
  { id: 'gills', label: 'Fish gills and countercurrent flow' },
  { id: 'tracheae', label: 'Insect tracheae' },
  { id: 'lungs', label: 'Mammal lungs' },
  { id: 'other', label: 'Other solutions' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world connections' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'model-limits', label: 'What the model simplifies' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Section 39.1: Systems of Gas Exchange. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/39-1-systems-of-gas-exchange' },
  { text: 'Schmidt-Nielsen, K. (1997). Animal Physiology: Adaptation and Environment (5th ed.). Cambridge University Press.' },
  { text: 'Hughes, G. M. (1966). The dimensions of fish gills in relation to their function. Journal of Experimental Biology, 45, 177–195.' },
  { text: 'Weibel, E. R. (1984). The Pathway for Oxygen: Structure and Function in the Mammalian Respiratory System. Harvard University Press.' },
  { text: 'Harrison, J. F., Kaiser, A., & VandenBrooks, J. M. (2010). Atmospheric oxygen level and the evolution of insect body size. Proceedings of the Royal Society B, 277, 1937–1946.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="zoology"
  topic="gas-exchange"
  description="Interactive gas exchange simulator: compare fish gills with countercurrent flow, insect tracheae and mammal lungs, change oxygen availability, surface area and barrier thickness, and watch diffusion follow Fick's law."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={gasExchangeQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      A trout, a grasshopper and a horse all need a steady supply of oxygen, but they get it in completely different ways:
      through feathery gills bathed in water, through a maze of air tubes, or through spongy lungs. Change the oxygen supply,
      the size of the exchange surface and the thickness of the barrier, and see how each design keeps its owner alive.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <GasExchangeSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Choose an <strong>animal</strong> and press <strong>Start</strong>. Watch water or air flow through its respiratory organ, and click each structure to learn what it does.</li>
      <li>In the <strong>close-up</strong>, red dots are oxygen and grey dots carbon dioxide. Watch more molecules cross one way than the other: diffusion down a gradient.</li>
      <li>Change the <strong>oxygen available</strong>, the <strong>surface area</strong> and the <strong>barrier thickness</strong>, then switch on <strong>Active</strong> (or <strong>Flying</strong>) and compare the oxygen uptake.</li>
      <li>For the fish, switch between <strong>countercurrent</strong> and <strong>parallel</strong> flow and compare the graph below. For the insect, increase the <strong>body size</strong>.</li>
      <li>The <strong>Fick’s law</strong> panel summarises the factors. <strong>Reset</strong> returns to the fish at rest.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="why">Why animals need gas exchange</h2>
  <p>
    Animal cells release energy from food by aerobic respiration, which uses oxygen and produces carbon dioxide.
    <strong>Gas exchange</strong> is the process of taking in oxygen from the surroundings and getting rid of carbon dioxide.
    In very small animals, gases diffuse directly through the body surface. Larger animals have a smaller surface area
    compared with their volume, so they need specialised <strong>respiratory surfaces</strong>, and usually a way of moving
    water or air over them (ventilation) and a circulatory system to carry gases to and from the cells.
  </p>

  <h2 id="diffusion">Diffusion and Fick’s law</h2>
  <p>
    Gases cross respiratory surfaces by <strong>diffusion</strong>: molecules move randomly, and more of them move from where
    they are concentrated to where they are scarce. The difference in concentration, or for gases in
    <strong>partial pressure</strong>, is the <strong>gradient</strong>. <strong>Fick’s law</strong> says the rate of diffusion
    is proportional to the surface area and the gradient, and inversely proportional to the thickness of the barrier:
  </p>
  <code class="formula">rate of diffusion ∝ surface area × difference in partial pressure ÷ thickness</code>
  <p>
    So efficient respiratory surfaces are <strong>large</strong>, <strong>thin</strong> and <strong>moist</strong> (gases must
    dissolve before they can cross cells), and breathing and blood flow keep the gradient <strong>steep</strong> by constantly
    bringing fresh water or air and carrying the oxygen-loaded blood away.
  </p>

  <h2 id="water-air">Water versus air</h2>
  <p>
    A litre of air contains about 210 mL of oxygen. A litre of water contains only about 5–10 mL, and less when it is warm
    or salty. Oxygen also diffuses about ten thousand times more slowly in water, and water is about 800 times denser than
    air and much more viscous, so pumping it over a respiratory surface takes far more energy. Breathing air is easier, but
    has a different problem: moist respiratory surfaces lose water by evaporation, so air-breathers keep them inside the
    body.
  </p>

  <h2 id="gills">Fish gills and countercurrent flow</h2>
  <p>
    A bony fish has four gill arches on each side, each carrying two rows of <strong>filaments</strong> covered in thin plates
    called <strong>lamellae</strong>, giving a huge surface area. The fish pumps water in through its mouth and out under its
    gill cover (operculum), so water flows over the gills in one direction. In each lamella, blood flows in the
    <strong>opposite direction</strong> to the water. This <strong>countercurrent exchange</strong> means blood always meets
    water containing a little more oxygen than itself, so a gradient exists all the way along, and fish can remove about
    80% of the oxygen from the water. If both flowed the same way, their oxygen levels would quickly even out and exchange
    would stop. Gills collapse and stick together out of water, which is why most fish suffocate in air.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tracheae">Insect tracheae</h2>
  <p>
    Insects have no lungs and their blood carries little oxygen. Instead, air enters through <strong>spiracles</strong>,
    small openings along the body that can be closed by valves to save water. These lead into <strong>tracheae</strong>,
    air-filled tubes reinforced with spiral rings of cuticle, which branch into microscopic <strong>tracheoles</strong> ending
    right next to the cells. Oxygen diffuses along the tubes; active insects also pump air by squeezing their abdomen. This
    system works brilliantly over short distances, and is one reason insects are small. About 300 million years ago, when
    the air held more oxygen, giant dragonfly relatives had wingspans of around 70 cm.
  </p>

  <h2 id="lungs">Mammal lungs</h2>
  <p>
    Air enters through the nose and mouth, passes down the <strong>trachea</strong> and into two <strong>bronchi</strong>,
    which branch repeatedly into bronchioles ending in hundreds of millions of tiny air sacs, the <strong>alveoli</strong>.
    Their total surface is about 70 m² in an adult human, and the barrier between air and blood is only about half a
    micrometre thick. Breathing is driven by the <strong>diaphragm</strong> and rib muscles, which enlarge the chest so air
    flows in. Air flows in and out the same way (tidal breathing), so some stale air always remains, and the air in the
    alveoli contains less oxygen than outside air.
  </p>

  <h2 id="other">Other solutions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Birds</strong> have air sacs that push air through their lungs in one direction, so the lungs are always ventilated with fresh air.</li>
    <li><strong>Frogs and earthworms</strong> exchange gases through their moist skin.</li>
    <li><strong>Tadpoles</strong> use gills, then switch to lungs and skin as adult frogs.</li>
    <li><strong>Lungfish</strong> have both gills and lungs and can gulp air when water is low in oxygen.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world connections</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fish kills:</strong> in hot summers, warm, still water holds less oxygen, and fish can suffocate.</li>
    <li><strong>High altitude:</strong> thinner air means a smaller gradient, so climbers breathe harder and their bodies make more red blood cells.</li>
    <li><strong>Lung diseases</strong> such as emphysema (less surface area) and pulmonary fibrosis (thicker barrier) reduce gas exchange, especially during exercise.</li>
    <li><strong>Aquaculture</strong> farms aerate their ponds to keep oxygen levels high.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Fish breathe water.”</strong> They extract the oxygen dissolved in water; they do not use the oxygen atoms in water molecules.</li>
    <li><strong>“Breathing and respiration are the same.”</strong> Breathing moves air or water; respiration releases energy in cells.</li>
    <li><strong>“We breathe out only carbon dioxide.”</strong> Exhaled air still contains about 16% oxygen.</li>
    <li><strong>“All animals have lungs.”</strong> Gills, tracheae and skin are just as successful in their own environments.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the model simplifies</h2>
  <p>
    The exchange calculations use Fick’s law and a standard oxygen-haemoglobin curve, but the units, flows and transit times
    are illustrative, not measurements of a real trout, grasshopper or human. The fish model treats one lamella as a pair of
    opposing streams; the lung model treats alveolar air as perfectly mixed; the insect model reduces the tracheal system to
    a single diffusion path, with pumping shortening it during flight. The close-up shows a small number of molecules
    wandering randomly; real numbers are astronomically larger. Carbon dioxide is shown only qualitatively, and the
    drawings are schematic and not to scale.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Oxygen and carbon dioxide cross respiratory surfaces by diffusion down partial-pressure gradients.</li>
    <li>Fick’s law: a large, thin, moist surface and a steep gradient give fast exchange.</li>
    <li>Fish gills use countercurrent flow to extract most of the scarce oxygen in water.</li>
    <li>Insects deliver air directly to their cells through tracheae, which limits their size.</li>
    <li>Mammal lungs have a huge alveolar surface with a very thin barrier, ventilated by tidal breathing.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
